nvk-zcull: hierarchical Z (ZCULL) over NVRM.
NullMoth's own change (not an upstream Mesa commit).

NVK 26.3 already drives the GPU's ZCULL unit, which throws away fragments hidden behind depth already drawn before
they are shaded: an image with only depth-attachment and read-only usages gets a ZCULL plane (nvk_image.c), and a
render pass whose depth attachment has such a plane, or is cleared at load, programs the unit (nvk_cmd_draw.c). It
does so only when the device reports the unit's geometry (nv_device_info.has_zcull_info, filled on Linux by
DRM_NOUVEAU_GET_ZCULL_INFO). The NVRM backend set it to false, so ZCULL never ran on macOS. It now asks RM for the same
figures, NV2080_CTRL_CMD_GR_GET_ZCULL_INFO and the ZCULL engine's context properties, as Linux's GSP code
(r570_gr_get_ctxbufs_and_zcull_info) does. Every graphics channel already binds a ZCULL context-switch buffer
SEPARATE_BUFFER (NVRM_610_ZCULL, nvkmd_nvrm_ctx.c), so the unit's state survives a switch to another process.

Off, as in 3.0.16, with NVK_ZCULL=0 (settings file or environment), in WindowServer, with NVRM_610_ZCULL=0, when either
RM call fails, or when a figure is one NIL would divide by or index past. NVK_DIAG=1 or NVK_ZCULL_LOG=1 writes one
line with the decision and the figures. tools/test_nvk_zcull.py.

